\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Reindexing and splitting reference volume}
\date{2026-10-25}

\p{For a bijection #{e:V\to W}, define
#{U_e:X_V\to X_W} by #{(U_ex)(w)=x(e^{-1}w)}. It is a linear
homeomorphism, with inverse #{U_{e^{-1}}}, preserving the counting
pairing, and
##{(U_e)_*\lambda_V=\lambda_W,\qquad
 f_*\mu(B):=\mu(f^{-1}B).}
For a disjoint decomposition #{V=R\sqcup D}, the restriction-pair map
#{\sigma(x)=(x|_R,x|_D)} has inverse pasting and satisfies
##{\sigma_*\lambda_V=\lambda_R\otimes\lambda_D.}
Either factor may be empty, carrying singleton mass one.}
\proof{
\p{The displayed maps and inverses are linear, hence continuous by
\ref{fdip-002C}. Reindexing the finite sum proves preservation of
#{h_V}. For a target rectangle #{\prod_wA_w}, its preimage under
#{U_e} is #{\prod_vA_{e(v)}}. The rectangle formula and reindexing
of a finite product give equal measures. The same argument for
#{\sigma} combines the retained and removed coordinate boxes.
The target generating boxes have prescribed finite cube masses, so
\ref{fdip-002I} extends both equalities to all Borel sets.}
\p{An enumeration #{b:\{1,\ldots,n\}\to V} gives the chart
#{C_b(x)_i=x(b(i))}. It therefore transports #{\lambda_V} to ordinary
Borel Lebesgue measure on #{\mathbb R^n}; two charts differ by a
coordinate permutation. At #{n=0} both spaces are singletons with mass one.
This proves independence of enumeration without a determinant calculation.}
}
\p{These finite-coordinate proofs use the product convention of
\citet{Section 1.7.4, pp. 194-199, preliminary version}{tao2011measure}.
The corresponding sigma-finite APIs are
\leanref{MeasureTheory.measurePreserving_piCongrLeft} and
\leanref{MeasureTheory.measurePreserving_piEquivPiSubtypeProd}; the latter
uses a decidable splitting predicate. A one-coordinate split is
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.measurePreserving_piSplitAt}, and the specified
four-coordinate grouping is
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.measurePreserving_finFourArrowPairPair}.}
\p{Bare restriction #{\rho_R} is measurable, but if #{D\ne\varnothing}
it is not reference-preserving: for a Borel #{A\subseteq X_R} of finite
positive mass, #{\rho_R^{-1}(A)} has mass
#{\lambda_R(A)\lambda_D(X_D)=\infty}. This includes #{R=\varnothing},
when the target singleton has mass one. The probability-factor assumption
in \leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.measurePreserving_pi_comp_embedding} excludes
these unnormalized Lebesgue factors. Normalized marginals are a different
[measure statement](fdip-000E).}
